{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I understand you came in with chest pain earlier tonight. Can you tell me more about what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I was arguing with my son and suddenly felt a tight pain in my chest, like before I got my stent. It went down my left arm and into my back. I got short of breath and had a headache."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did the pain get better with anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I took three nitroglycerin tablets and some morphine in the ER. It helped. But I was also sweating a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We did an EKG and your cardiac enzymes were normal, which is reassuring. The EKG showed some minor changes, but they look similar to your old ones. That suggests it’s not a new heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart? But it felt just like last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s definitely concerning, especially with your history. But since your tests are negative and you’ve had similar pain before without a blockage, we think this episode was likely triggered by stress and not a new cardiac event."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. But I’ve been really stressed lately. My son has a mental disability and drinks a lot. He’s been verbally abusive and even tried to hit me once."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I’m sorry you’re going through that. We’ve involved social work, and they’ve spoken with you. I see you’re not comfortable going home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I don’t feel safe. I’d rather go to a shelter, or at least stay with my boyfriend for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2176-07-05 01:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll support that. Social work is helping you plan next steps, including filing a restraining order. Your safety is very important."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2176-07-05 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood sugar was low in the ER—43—but came up quickly with juice. Then this morning it was 232. Your HbA1c is 10, which shows your diabetes has been hard to control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2176-07-05 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I know. My sugars are usually between 200 and 300. I haven’t had a low like that in years."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2176-07-05 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep you on your insulin—Lantus and Humalog—as you were at home, and add a sliding scale to help manage highs. We’ll also check your sugars regularly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2176-07-05 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll try to do better. What about my cholesterol? I remember it’s always high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2176-07-05 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your triglycerides were over 500, but you’d just eaten to treat the low sugar, so we’ll need a fasting panel later. For now, keep taking your simvastatin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well overnight—no chest pain, stable vitals. We’re going to discharge you home today, but you’ll stay with your boyfriend, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, and I’ll go to court tomorrow for the restraining order."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Take all your medications as listed. If you have chest pain, trouble breathing, low blood sugar, or feel unsafe, come back right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll follow up with my cardiologist and primary doctor?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ll send the records to them. Keep managing your diabetes closely, and call if anything feels off."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better knowing I’m not having a heart attack and that I have a plan for home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2176-07-05 16:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care of yourself, and don’t hesitate to reach out."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2176-07-05 01:04:00",
    "admission_end": "2176-07-05 17:00:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of coronary artery disease, diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia was admitted for substernal chest pain radiating to the left arm and back, triggered by a stressful argument with her son. The pain resolved with nitroglycerin and morphine. Evaluation showed no acute ischemic changes on EKG and normal cardiac enzymes, indicating no myocardial infarction. Her symptoms were consistent with prior stress-related episodes. She was hypoglycemic on arrival (glucose 43), which resolved, but her HbA1c was elevated at 10.0, reflecting poor long-term diabetes control. Triglycerides were markedly elevated, though not fasting. Social work addressed her concerns about verbal and physical abuse from her son; she elected to stay with her boyfriend and pursue a restraining order. She remained stable, with no recurrence of chest pain, and was discharged home with continued cardiac and diabetes medications and follow-up plans.",
    "problems": [
      "Other chest pain",
      "Other adult abuse and neglect",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Assault by other specified means",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Lichenification and lichen simplex chronicus",
      "Other late effects of cerebrovascular disease, facial weakness",
      "Esophageal reflux"
    ]
  }
